from pathlib import Path
import json, html
root=Path('site/experiments/intelligence-explosion')
# Draft cards live in the private planning folder so unapproved text never ships.
cards_path=Path('planning/hub-cards.json')
record_path=root/'record.json'
if not cards_path.exists() and not record_path.exists():
 raise SystemExit('No card source found: expected planning/hub-cards.json or site/experiments/intelligence-explosion/record.json')
cards=json.loads((cards_path if cards_path.exists() else record_path).read_text())
esc=html.escape
approved={c['id']:c for c in cards if c['approved']}
record_path.write_text(json.dumps(list(approved.values()), indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
LINKS={'garicano-imas':'https://www.siliconcontinent.com/p/are-we-approaching-an-intelligence','chalmers':'https://x.com/davidchalmers42/status/2120551211195642338'}
def ref(id,text):
 return f'<a href="{("#"+id) if id in approved else esc(LINKS[id],quote=True)}">{text}</a>'
def card(c):
 fields=[('What they claim.','claim'),('Kind of evidence.','kind'),('Questions.','questions'),('Their caveats.','caveats'),('How it fits.','fits'),('How sure.','tag')]
 return '<details id="'+esc(c['id'],quote=True)+'"><summary>'+esc(c['title'])+' <span>'+esc(c['who'])+'</span></summary>'+''.join('<p><strong>'+label+'</strong> '+esc(c[key])+'</p>' for label,key in fields if c.get(key))+'<p><a href="'+esc(c['link'],quote=True)+'">Read the original →</a></p></details>'
source=Path('site/experiments/spending-puzzle/index.html').read_text(); header=source[:source.index('<header class="head">')].replace('The spending puzzle ·','Is there an intelligence explosion? ·').replace('href="../intelligence-explosion/">Overview','href="../intelligence-explosion/" aria-current="page">Overview').replace('href="../spending-puzzle/" aria-current="page"','href="../spending-puzzle/"').replace('href="styles.css','href="../spending-puzzle/styles.css')
header=header.replace('<body>', '<body class="hub">')
opening='An intelligence explosion would mean AI improving AI research so strongly that the growth rate of AI\'s abilities keeps rising, instead of settling at a new, faster pace. Researchers, economists and philosophers now disagree about whether one has begun. This page gathers the work worth reading on that question, explains it in plain words, and says how it fits together. We update it as new work appears.'
# Replace inherited social metadata with the hub's own opening.
import re
header=re.sub(r'<meta (?:name="description"|property="og:(?:title|description)")[^>]*>','',header)
header=header.replace('</head>',f'<meta name="description" content="{esc(opening,quote=True)}"><meta property="og:title" content="Is there an intelligence explosion?"><meta property="og:description" content="{esc(opening,quote=True)}"></head>')
g=ref('garicano-imas','Garicano and Imas'); ch=ref('chalmers','Chalmers')
body='''<header class="head">Economics of AI · 14.11.2026</header><main class="sheet"><section id="chapter-1"><h1>Is there an intelligence explosion?</h1>
<div class="noted"><p>'''+opening+'''</p><aside class="note"><strong>OUR GUESS</strong> "Worth reading" is our judgement.</aside></div>
<h3>Two ways research could go</h3>
<div class="noted"><p>Think of research as a curve. Along the bottom is the total effort put in so far: money, staff and computers. Up the side is progress on some measure of AI's abilities. In the fields economists have studied, the curve bends over: the easy ideas get found first, so each step costs more effort than the last. An explosion would need the curve to bend the other way, with AI's improvements feeding back into research strongly enough to outrun the rising difficulty. The feedback could come from AI doing more of the research, from AI earning money that pays for more research, or from both. How bent the curve looks also depends on how progress is measured.</p><aside class="note"><strong>ILLUSTRATION</strong> Hand-drawn shapes, not data. The first follows what <a href="https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20180338">Bloom and others</a> found in the fields they studied. The second follows models of feedback, such as <a href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w23928">Aghion, Jones and Jones</a> and <a href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35155">Davidson and others</a>.</aside></div>
<figure class="ie-curves"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 375" role="img" aria-labelledby="ie-curves-title"><title id="ie-curves-title">An illustration, not data: two curves of progress against total effort. One bends over, so each step costs more. The other bends upward, because AI feeds back into research strongly enough that each step makes the next cheaper.</title><path d="M50 20V330H505" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M50 330C150 165 300 115 500 95" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M50 330C250 320 400 240 470 20" fill="none" stroke="var(--red)" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="9 6"/><text x="60" y="80">Today: each step</text><text x="60" y="100">costs more than the last</text><text x="505" y="235" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--red)">If AI feeds back</text><text x="505" y="255" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--red)">strongly enough,</text><text x="505" y="275" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--red)">each step makes</text><text x="505" y="295" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--red)">the next cheaper</text><text x="505" y="362" text-anchor="end">Total effort so far →</text><text x="40" y="20" text-anchor="end" transform="rotate(-90 40 20)">Progress (some measure) →</text></svg></figure>
<p>Much of the work on these pages was done by Claude, an AI made by Anthropic, the company whose research is measured here. Read with that in mind. <a href="https://github.com/ashishefe/lfe-research-machine">The code and the text are open</a>.</p></section>
<section><h2>Where things stand</h2><p>Last updated 14 November 2026.</p>'''
items=[("AI research at Anthropic has sped up.","After October 2025, Anthropic's AI models improved about 1.9 times as fast as before, on METR's measure.",'<a href="https://metr.org/blog/2026-11-05-anthropic-ai-rd-acceleration-assessment/">METR</a>, 5 November 2026, on data Anthropic shared. Its estimate is 1.9 times, with a 95% range of 1.5 to 2.8.'),("Nobody yet knows how much of that came from more money and staff, and how much from AI tools.","Progress can speed up because more goes in, because more comes out of the same input, or both, and the two can feed each other: better AI can attract the money that buys more research. Anthropic spent far more, and each dollar bought less progress than before. The question is whether progress beat what the extra spending alone would predict. The model-based study discussed here, by Garicano and Imas, finds that it did, in part: more inputs explain some of the speedup, and the rest looks like a one-time boost, though they say that estimate is uncertain. A one-time boost is not an explosion, which needs the feedback to keep strengthening. Telling these apart is the hard part, and it is what these pages work through.",'<a href="https://www.siliconcontinent.com/p/are-we-approaching-an-intelligence">Garicano and Imas</a>, 13 November 2026. On their figures, progress per dollar fell about 69%.'),("In AI research, the easy ideas seem to be getting used up.","On Garicano and Imas's model, each step forward costs more than the last, as studies have found in many other fields. That rests on their assumptions, and how fast it is happening is uncertain.",'<a href="https://www.siliconcontinent.com/p/are-we-approaching-an-intelligence">Garicano and Imas</a> for AI. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20180338">Bloom and others</a> for other fields.'),("The public data show no clear sign yet of AI feeding its own research in a way that speeds up as AI improves.","That loop is what an explosion would need. AI is already helping with research, and METR, using evidence it could not publish, judges that AI accounts for much of the faster pace. The public figures can neither confirm nor rule that out.",'<a href="https://www.siliconcontinent.com/p/are-we-approaching-an-intelligence">Garicano and Imas</a>, from their model. <a href="https://metr.org/blog/2026-11-05-anthropic-ai-rd-acceleration-assessment/">METR</a> on AI\'s share.'),("It cannot be ruled out.","Daniel Kokotajlo, an author of the AI 2027 scenario, wrote that if METR is right, an explosion now looks likely in 2027. In Garicano and Imas's broadest test of measurement error, about one in eleven re-runs of their model meet its condition for an explosion. That shows how much the answer depends on noisy scores. It is not a probability, independent of their model, that an explosion has begun.",'<a href="https://x.com/DKokotajlo/status/2120204075803500595">Kokotajlo</a>, 10 November 2026, and the <a href="https://blog.aifutures.org/p/q35-timelines-update-looks-like-the">AI Futures post</a> he links. Garicano and Imas, <a href="https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/jz1zaj2fk741g2len97q9/Online-Appendix-to-Garicano-and-Imas-AI-ideas-getting-harder-to-find.pdf?rlkey=narofum32aiq105vrcwcx92vy&amp;dl=0">online appendix</a>, section F8.')]
for i,(a,b,c) in enumerate(items,1): body+=f'<div class="noted"><p>{i}. <strong>{esc(a)}</strong> {esc(b)}</p><aside class="note"><strong>CLAIMED</strong> {c}</aside></div>'
body+='''<div class="noted"><p class="summary-note-row" aria-hidden="true"></p><aside class="note"><strong>OUR GUESS</strong> This summary is ours. It will change as new work appears.</aside></div></section><section><h2>The questions inside the question</h2>
<h3>Is AI research speeding up?</h3><p>Yes, at Anthropic, by about 1.9 times on one measure, with a wide range. → <a href="https://metr.org/blog/2026-11-05-anthropic-ai-rd-acceleration-assessment/">METR</a>.</p>
<h3>How much of the speedup is money, and how much is AI?</h3><p>Unsettled. The answer depends on what you assume would have happened anyway. → <a href="https://x.com/sebkrier/status/2119182834162340095">Krier</a> and <a href="https://x.com/michael_wiebe/status/2119207429393220041">Wiebe</a>; '''+g+'''; <a href="../spending-puzzle/">our spending puzzle</a>.</p>
<h3>Are ideas getting harder to find in AI?</h3><p>Probably, on Garicano and Imas's estimates. How fast, and whether the rate is changing, is uncertain. → '''+g+'''.</p>
<h3>Is AI starting to do its own research?</h3><p>AI already helps with parts of research. The public data show no clear sign yet of the self-feeding kind. David Chalmers asks a related question: the gains so far may be in the easier parts of research, such as coding and running experiments, and could grow fast there without reaching harder parts such as research judgement. → '''+g+'; '+ch+'''.</p>
<h3>What would an explosion need, and what would we see first?</h3><p>AI that gets better at the hard parts of research, not only the easy ones, which is Chalmers's question. And feedback, through better research tools or through money, strong enough that returns to research effort stop falling and start to rise, which is the condition in Garicano and Imas's model. → '''+ch+'; '+g+'''.</p>
<h3>What would reduce the uncertainty?</h3><div class="noted"><p>Model-by-model scores with their uncertainty, the research bill broken into its parts, how long spending takes to show up, and comparisons fixed in advance. → <a href="../spending-puzzle/#chapter-7">our spending puzzle, section 7</a>.</p><aside class="note"><strong>OUR GUESS</strong> The answers in this section are our reading of the work linked.</aside></div></section>
'''
end='<footer class="colophon">Built by Ashish Kulkarni with Claude and Codex. Claude is made by Anthropic. <a href="https://github.com/ashishefe/lfe-research-machine">Code and text are open.</a> Built to be argued with. Design craft learned from Keshav Jindal.</footer></main><script src="app.js"></script></body></html>'
record_open='<section><h2>The record</h2><p>Each entry: who and when, what they claim, the kind of evidence, and how it fits with the rest. Newest first.</p><div id="record">'
record_close='</div></section>'
(root/'index.html').write_text(header+body+((record_open+''.join(card(c) for c in approved.values())+record_close) if approved else '')+end)
# Preview lives outside the public site; absolute file URLs retain local assets and links.
preview=(header+body+record_open+''.join(card(c) for c in cards)+record_close+end).replace('<head>','<head><base href="'+root.resolve().as_uri()+'/">')
Path('dist').mkdir(exist_ok=True); Path('dist/hub-drafts-preview.html').write_text(preview)
